(project tuna-grants
 (doc "Tuna book chapter: grants — the capability model. Nothing inside the calculus is resolvable by name; a run touches the world only through grant tokens physically passed to it as tree arguments.")
 (status planned)
 (convention ocaml-dune)
 (section grants
  (doc "Capability grants as the security model: grants are arguments, the evaluator guarantees reachability, the host defines policy.")

  (subsection grant-token
   (doc "Shape of a grant: an opaque row the evaluator never interprets")
   (status planned)
   (details
    (doc (|
         A grant is a store row:

           grants (id uuid PK,
                   prim text,               -- which primitive it unlocks
                   args_attenuation jsonb,  -- host-interpreted narrowing predicate
                   caller text,             -- identity the grant is attributable to
                   minted_by uuid NULL,     -- parent grant for derived grants
                   created_at timestamptz,
                   revoked_at timestamptz NULL)

         To the calculus a grant is inert tree data — an opaque node
         passed as a run argument. The evaluator never resolves a name,
         never reads the store, never interprets the predicate. Its one
         guarantee is reachability: a run can reference exactly what is
         physically passed to it, nothing else. How args_attenuation is
         scoped and whether scoping is CORRECT is host policy and stays
         a review matter; this is a hard non-goal of the evaluator
         (SPEC.md §7) and the docs must not claim otherwise.

         Open decision (v0's questions, carried): bearer-style token vs
         row id. v1 chooses ROW IDs: the row is inspectable, revocable,
         and attributable; the bearer trade (tamper-proof standalone
         tokens) buys nothing inside a daemon that always checks the row
         anyway.
    |))
   )
  )

  (subsection invocation
   (doc "How a run spends a grant at the boundary")
   (status planned)
   (details
    (doc (|
         Run submission carries: program hash, input tree hashes, fuel,
         size cap, and a list of grant ids. The host injects the grant
         trees as arguments; the program's prim-call nodes reference
         them structurally.

         At each boundary event (journal.row-schema) the host checks the
         grant row LIVE: exists, not revoked, attenuation admits the
         call args. Denied calls are journaled as error results with a
         denial reason and the run continues per program semantics —
         denial is data, never an ambient exception escaping the
         calculus. Fuel accounting is unaffected: a denied call is a
         normal boundary event.

         Grant check results are NOT journaled as replay inputs — during
         faithful replay the journal answers prim calls (replay.faithful)
         and no live check runs. Revocation therefore cannot retroactively
         alter history, and replay correctness never depends on grant
         lifecycle state.
    |))
   )
  )

  (subsection revocation
   (doc "Revocation semantics: immediate, forward-only")
   (status planned)
   (details
    (doc (|
         Revocation is a row update setting revoked_at. Effects:

           - Next live boundary check fails; in-flight runs observe the
             denial at their next prim call. No pre-emptive abort: the
             program decides how to handle the journaled denial.
           - Recorded runs are immutable history; revocation never
             mutates journals or run rows.
           - Faithful replay is unaffected (journal answers, not grants).

         Open question: whether long-running runs should receive a
         revocation NOTIFICATION prim (host pushes an event the run can
         poll) or remain purely pull-based. Pull-only is simpler and
         journal-clean; v1 stays pull-only unless a use case forces it.
    |))
   )
  )

  (subsection delegation-attenuation
   (doc "Minting narrower grants from broader ones")
   (status planned)
   (details
    (doc (|
         Delegation is structural: a grant tree is data and may be passed
         downward to any subprogram as a normal argument. Attenuation is
         HOST-side: deriving a narrower grant requires a mint-grant call
         by a caller whose own grant admits it; the derived row records
         minted_by, so grant lineage is queryable.

         v1 explicitly excludes: granting capabilities FROM INSIDE the
         calculus (no prim-call that mints; minting is a host API
         operation only) and object-capability style sealers/unsealers.
         Both are expressible later as ordinary prim design, not calculus
         changes — that layering claim is part of what the project tests.
    |))
   )
  )

  (subsection audit
   (doc "Grant usage as a SQL query — the audit demo")
   (status planned)
   (details
    (doc (|
         Because every journal row carries grant id, prim, callsite
         path, and caller, "which runs touched grant g" (and "what did
         grant g's lineage do, ever") is a plain SQL query over journals
         joined to run rows. No audit log reconciliation, no side
         channel: the effect record IS the audit record.

         This is the chapter's headline acceptance demo and the concrete
         answer to "why structure over text": in a text-seam system this
         query is a log-grep heuristic; here it is indexed relational
         data produced as a byproduct of running.
    |))
   )
  )
 )
)
